ELETE` is irreversible.\n3. **Do not fix live-lock residue by silencing the leak audit.** Record the exact namespace, lock name, and holder pid, and keep the gate red until it clears and is drained.\n4. **Do not hand-edit generated/merged status artifacts.** Status is derivable; reconcile against observation and fail on drift.\n5. **Do not treat a missing/`null` field as \"no change.\"** On a shared schema a `null` PATCH is destructive: 422 it, and deep-merge null-safely.\n\n**Bottom line:** the fresh named-substrate-lock preflight (`preflight_worker.py`) refuses the battery when a lock is named and live, returns live-lock residue in 0 s while the ordinary 61 s teardown ladder is preserved intact, refuses destructive null PATCHes, reconciles domain status drift, and aggregates every sub-check so that a partial gate is never called green.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: python-live-substrate-lock-preflight\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-24T19:29:13.926Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Auger foreman recovery found a landed prior fix, but the full gate still red due to shared DuckBrain schema null PATCHes, live-lock teardown residue, and domain status drift. The next actionable row is a fresh preflight worker for named substrate-lock detection; preserve 61s ordinary teardown backoff and do not call partial gates green.", "environment": "", "language": "", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "python-live-substrate-lock-preflight", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-24T19:29:13.930Z", "version": ""}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-24 19:29:13"}
+{"class_id": 2970, "class": "go-flexible-paxos-fast-quorum-recovery-ballot-safety", "title": "go-flexible-paxos-fast-quorum-recovery-ballot-safety", "description": "go-flexible-paxos-fast-quorum-recovery-ballot-safety", "answer_id": 4702, "language": "go", "environment": "go1.26", "version": "1.26", "solution": "# Flexible Paxos: safe independent Q1/Q2 quorums and crash recovery\n\nThe complete verified solution is also available at `/workspace/SOLUTION.md`, with source in `/workspace/flexpaxos`.\n\n## Root-cause analysis\n\nA normal Paxos implementation hard-codes “majority” in two places: it accepts a phase-1 quorum if `len(promises) >= N/2+1`, and a phase-2 quorum if `len(accepted) >= N/2+1`. That is wrong for Flexible Paxos.\n\n* The safety invariant is **intersection**, not majority: every phase-1 quorum `Q1` must intersect every phase-2 quorum `Q2`. If the quorums are “any subset of the configured sizes”, this is equivalent to `|Q1| + |Q2| > N`.\n* A fixed majority check is **unsound** when the configured `Q1` or `Q2` is smaller than a majority. Example: `N=5, Q1=1, Q2=5` is safe (the single Q1 member is in every Q2), but a hard-coded majority would reject the legal Q1.\n* A fixed majority check is also **needlessly rejecting** when the configured quorum is larger than a majority. Example: `N=5, Q1=4, Q2=2` is legal, but a hard-coded Q2 majority check rejects a valid Q2 of two acceptors.\n* Recovery after a proposer crash must not simply reuse its old value. Phase 1 must collect the accepted state from a `Q1`; because every `Q1` intersects the `Q2` that may have committed, at least one promise carries the committed value (or a newer accepted value). The proposer must choose the value with the **highest accepted ballot** among the promises before entering phase 2.\n* Stale/reordered messages are handled by making ballots totally ordered and by including the ballot (and log index) in every reply. An acceptor rejects an accept below its promise; a proposer discards replies for any ballot other than its current attempt. Duplicate phase-1 requests are idempotent.\n* Ballots must never be reused, even across a process crash. `BallotGen` must be restored from durable state (or from the maximum ballot observed/persisted) before issuing a new ballot. Reusing a ballot with a different value can break safety because two Q2 quorums need not intersect.\n\nThe fix is therefore:\n\n1. Validate `N`, `Q1`, `Q2` and reject `Q1+Q2 <= N` in the config loader.\n2. Persist the acceptor’s `promised`, `acceptedBallot`, `acceptedValue`, and recovery ballot.\n3. Count only the configured `Q1`/`Q2` sizes.\n4. On phase 1, recover the highest accepted value from the promises.\n5. Fence all stale traffic with the ballot and never reuse a ballot.\n\n## Exact fix\n\nCreate the module and files below:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p flexpaxos\ncd flexpaxos\ngo mod init example.com/flexpaxos\n```\n\n### `ballot.go`\n\n```go\npackage flexpaxos\n\nimport \"sync\"\n\n// Ballot is a totally ordered Paxos ballot. Round is the primary ordering key;\n// ID breaks ties between proposers if a deployment gives each proposer its own\n// counter. The harness uses one global generator so Round alone is unique.\ntype Ballot struct {\n\tRound uint64\n\tID    uint64\n}\n\nfunc (b Ballot) Compare(o Ballot) int {\n\tif b.Round < o.Round {\n\t\treturn -1\n\t}\n\tif b.Round > o.Round {\n\t\treturn 1\n\t}\n\tif b.ID < o.ID {\n\t\treturn -1\n\t}\n\tif b.ID > o.ID {\n\t\treturn 1\n\t}\n\treturn 0\n}\n\nfunc maxBallot(a, b Ballot) Ballot {\n\tif a.Compare(b) >= 0 {\n\t\treturn a\n\t}\n\treturn b\n}\n\n// BallotGen issues ballots strictly greater than a supplied floor. A real\n// deployment must persist last (or recover it from durable acceptor promises)\n// before issuing ballots after a process restart.\ntype BallotGen struct {\n\tmu   sync.Mutex\n\tlast uint64\n}\n\n// NewBallotGen restores a generator from durable state. Pass the greatest\n// ballot known to have been issued before a restart. A fresh cluster may use\n// NewBallotGen(0).\nfunc NewBallotGen(last uint64) *BallotGen { return &BallotGen{last: last} }\n\n// Last returns the greatest round issued/observed so far. Persist this value\n// before acknowledging a proposal if the same process may restart.\nfunc (g *BallotGen) Last() uint64 {\n\tg.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer g.mu.Unlock()\n\treturn g.last\n}\n\nfunc (g *BallotGen) Next(after Ballot) Ballot {\n\tg.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer g.mu.Unlock()\n\tif after.Round > g.last {\n\t\tg.last = after.Round\n\t}\n\tg.last++\n\treturn Ballot{Round: g.last, ID: 0}\n}\n```\n\n### `config.go`\n\n```go\npackage flexpaxos\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"os\"\n)\n\n// Config describes N acceptors and the sizes of the phase-1 and phase-2\n// quorums. Legal configurations satisfy |Q1|+|Q2| > N. That inequality is\n// exactly what guarantees every possible Q1 intersects every possible Q2.\ntype Config struct {\n\tN  int `json:\"n\"`\n\tQ1 int `json:\"q1\"`\n\tQ2 int `json:\"q2\"`\n}\n\nfunc (c Config) Validate() error {\n\tif c.N <= 0 {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"bad config: n must be > 0, got %d\", c.N)\n\t}\n\tif c.Q1 < 1 || c.Q1 > c.N {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"bad config: q1 must be in [1,%d], got %d\", c.N, c.Q1)\n\t}\n\tif c.Q2 < 1 || c.Q2 > c.N {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"bad config: q2 must be in [1,%d], got %d\", c.N, c.Q2)\n\t}\n\tif c.Q1+c.Q2 <= c.N {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"unsafe flexible quorum: need q1+q2 > n, got %d+%d <= %d\", c.Q1, c.Q2, c.N)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// LoadConfig reads a strict JSON config and rejects any unsafe quorum\n// configuration at the boundary, before a replica can be started.\nfunc LoadConfig(path string) (Config, error) {\n\tb, err := os.ReadFile(path)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn Config{}, err\n\t}\n\tvar c Config\n\tdec := json.NewDecoder(bytes.NewReader(b))\n\tdec.DisallowUnknownFields()\n\tif err := dec.Decode(&c); err != nil {\n\t\treturn Config{}, fmt.Errorf(\"parse config: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tif err := dec.Decode(&struct{}{}); err != io.EOF {\n\t\treturn Config{}, fmt.Errorf(\"parse config: trailing data after JSON object\")\n\t}\n\tif err := c.Validate(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn Config{}, err\n\t}\n\treturn c, nil\n}\n```\n\n### `acceptor.go`\n\n```go\npackage flexpaxos\n\nimport \"sync\"\n\n// Value is the replicated value. A separate bool records whether an acceptor\n// has ever accepted a value, so the empty string is a legal value.\ntype Value string\n\n// PrepareReply is the durable information an acceptor returns in phase 1.\ntype PrepareReply struct {\n\tFrom           int\n\tBallot         Ballot\n\tOK             bool\n\tPromised       Ballot\n\tAcceptedBallot Ballot\n\tAcceptedValue  Value\n\tHasAccepted    bool\n}\n\n// AcceptReply confirms a phase-2 write.\ntype AcceptReply struct {\n\tFrom     int\n\tBallot   Ballot\n\tOK       bool\n\tPromised Ballot\n}\n\n// Acceptor is a persistent Flexible Paxos acceptor. promised, acceptedBallot,\n// acceptedValue and recoveryBallot are the state that must survive a crash.\n// recoveryBallot is the highest ballot for which this acceptor has joined a\n// recovery (phase-1) round; it is diagnostic/lease state, while safety comes\n// from promised + acceptedBallot/value.\ntype Acceptor struct {\n\tID int\n\n\tmu             sync.Mutex\n\tpromised       Ballot\n\thasPromised    bool\n\tacceptedBallot Ballot\n\tacceptedValue  Value\n\thasAccepted    bool\n\trecoveryBallot Ballot\n\thasRecovery    bool\n}\n\nfunc NewAcceptor(id int) *Acceptor { return &Acceptor{ID: id} }\n\n// Prepare promises not to accept any ballot below b and reports the value (if\n// any) already accepted by this acceptor. Equal ballots are idempotent, which\n// lets a retried prepare be safe under duplicate/reordered messages.\nfunc (a *Acceptor) Prepare(b Ballot) PrepareReply {\n\ta.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer a.mu.Unlock()\n\n\tif a.hasPromised && b.Compare(a.promised) < 0 {\n\t\treturn PrepareReply{From: a.ID, Ballot: b, OK: false, Promised: a.promised}\n\t}\n\ta.promised = maxBallot(a.promised, b)\n\ta.hasPromised = true\n\ta.recoveryBallot = maxBallot(a.recoveryBallot, b)\n\ta.hasRecovery = true\n\treturn PrepareReply{\n\t\tFrom:           a.ID,\n\t\tBallot:         b,\n\t\tOK:             true,\n\t\tPromised:       a.promised,\n\t\tAcceptedBallot: a.acceptedBallot,\n\t\tAcceptedValue:  a.acceptedValue,\n\t\tHasAccepted:    a.hasAccepted,\n\t}\n}\n\n// Accept accepts b/value only if no higher ballot has been promised. Accepting\n// a second, different value at the same ballot is refused (proposer bug or\n// Byzantine input); a correct proposer retries with a strictly newer ballot.\nfunc (a *Acceptor) Accept(b Ballot, v Value) AcceptReply {\n\ta.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer a.mu.Unlock()\n\n\tif a.hasPromised && b.Compare(a.promised) < 0 {\n\t\treturn AcceptReply{From: a.ID, Ballot: b, OK: false, Promised: a.promised}\n\t}\n\tif a.hasAccepted && b.Compare(a.acceptedBallot) == 0 && v != a.acceptedValue {\n\t\treturn AcceptReply{From: a.ID, Ballot: b, OK: false, Promised: a.promised}\n\t}\n\ta.promised = maxBallot(a.promised, b)\n\ta.hasPromised = true\n\ta.acceptedBallot = b\n\ta.acceptedValue = v\n\ta.hasAccepted = true\n\treturn AcceptReply{From: a.ID, Ballot: b, OK: true, Promised: a.promised}\n}\n\n// Snapshot is for diagnostics and assertions; it is not used by the protocol.\nfunc (a *Acceptor) Snapshot() (promised Ballot, acceptedBallot Ballot, acceptedValue Value, hasAccepted bool, recovery Ballot) {\n\ta.mu.Lock()\n\tdefer a.mu.Unlock()\n\treturn a.promised, a.acceptedBallot, a.acceptedValue, a.hasAccepted, a.recoveryBallot\n}\n```\n\n### `proposer.go`\n\n```go\npackage flexpaxos\n\n// MsgType is the wire type used by the deterministic harness.\ntype MsgType uint8\n\nconst (\n\tMsgPrepare MsgType = iota\n\tMsgPromise\n\tMsgAccept\n\tMsgAccepted\n)\n\n// Message is both a request and a response. For MsgPrepare/MsgAccept, From is\n// the proposer ID and To is the acceptor ID. For replies it is the reverse.\ntype Message struct {\n\tType           MsgType\n\tFrom, To       int\n\tIndex          int\n\tBallot         Ballot\n\tValue          Value\n\tOK             bool\n\tPromised       Ballot\n\tAcceptedBallot Ballot\n\tAcceptedValue  Value\n\tHasAccepted    bool\n}\n\ntype phase uint8\n\nconst (\n\tphaseIdle phase = iota\n\tphasePreparing\n\tphaseAccepting\n\tphaseCommitted\n\tphaseAborted\n)\n\n// Proposer implements the Flexible Paxos proposer. A new attempt always uses a\n// strictly larger ballot. Phase 1 waits for Q1 promises, selects the accepted\n// value with the highest accepted ballot among those promises (falling back to\n// the proposal value), and only then sends phase 2. A commit is declared when\n// Q2 distinct acceptors confirm the same ballot.\ntype Proposer struct {\n\tID      int\n\tConfig  Config\n\tBallots *BallotGen\n\n\tindex       int\n\tinitial     Value\n\tballot      Ballot\n\tphase       phase\n\tpromises    map[int]PrepareReply\n\taccepts     map[int]struct{}\n\tchosen      Value\n\tcommitted   bool\n\tcommitValue Value\n\tattempts    int\n\tmaxAttempts int\n}\n\nfunc NewProposer(id int, cfg Config, ballots *BallotGen) *Proposer {\n\treturn &Proposer{\n\t\tID:          id,\n\t\tConfig:      cfg,\n\t\tBallots:     ballots,\n\t\tmaxAttempts: 16,\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc (p *Proposer) Committed() (Value, bool) { return p.commitValue, p.committed }\nfunc (p *Proposer) Ballot() Ballot           { return p.ballot }\nfunc (p *Proposer) Phase() phase             { return p.phase }\n\n// Start begins a fresh proposal for one log index.\nfunc (p *Proposer) Start(index int, value Value) []Message {\n\tp.index = index\n\tp.initial = value\n\tp.attempts = 0\n\tp.committed = false\n\tp.commitValue = \"\"\n\tp.phase = phaseIdle\n\treturn p.newAttempt(Ballot{})\n}\n\nfunc (p *Proposer) newAttempt(after Ballot) []Message {\n\tif p.attempts >= p.maxAttempts {\n\t\tp.phase = phaseAborted\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tp.attempts++\n\tp.ballot = p.Ballots.Next(after)\n\tp.phase = phasePreparing\n\tp.promises = make(map[int]PrepareReply)\n\tp.accepts = make(map[int]struct{})\n\tp.chosen = p.initial\n\tmsgs := make([]Message, 0, p.Config.N)\n\tfor i := 0; i < p.Config.N; i++ {\n\t\tmsgs = append(msgs, Message{\n\t\t\tType: MsgPrepare, From: p.ID, To: i, Index: p.index, Ballot: p.ballot,\n\t\t})\n\t}\n\treturn msgs\n}\n\n// Handle consumes one reply. Replies from an older ballot or from a previous\n// attempt are ignored, so duplicated/reordered/stale traffic cannot corrupt\n// the current attempt.\nfunc (p *Proposer) Handle(m Message) []Message {\n\tif m.Index != p.index || m.Ballot.Compare(p.ballot) != 0 {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tswitch p.phase {\n\tcase phasePreparing:\n\t\tif m.Type != MsgPromise {\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tif !m.OK {\n\t\t\tif m.Promised.Compare(p.ballot) > 0 {\n\t\t\t\treturn p.newAttempt(maxBallot(p.ballot, m.Promised))\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tif _, seen := p.promises[m.From]; seen {\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tp.promises[m.From] = PrepareReply{\n\t\t\tFrom: m.From, Ballot: m.Ballot, OK: true, Promised: m.Promised,\n\t\t\tAcceptedBallot: m.AcceptedBallot, AcceptedValue: m.AcceptedValue,\n\t\t\tHasAccepted: m.HasAccepted,\n\t\t}\n\t\tif len(p.promises) < p.Config.Q1 {\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t// Recovery rule: take the value accepted at the highest ballot in Q1.\n\t\tp.chosen = p.initial\n\t\tvar bestBallot Ballot\n\t\tfound := false\n\t\tfor _, r := range p.promises {\n\t\t\tif !r.HasAccepted {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif !found || r.AcceptedBallot.Compare(bestBallot) > 0 {\n\t\t\t\tfound = true\n\t\t\t\tbestBallot = r.AcceptedBallot\n\t\t\t\tp.chosen = r.AcceptedValue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\n\t\tp.phase = phaseAccepting\n\t\tmsgs := make([]Message, 0, p.Config.N)\n\t\tfor i := 0; i < p.Config.N; i++ {\n\t\t\tmsgs = append(msgs, Message{\n\t\t\t\tType: MsgAccept, From: p.ID, To: i, Index: p.index,\n\t\t\t\tBallot: p.ballot, Value: p.chosen,\n\t\t\t})\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn msgs\n\n\tcase phaseAccepting:\n\t\tif m.Type != MsgAccepted {\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tif !m.OK {\n\t\t\tif m.Promised.Compare(p.ballot) > 0 {\n\t\t\t\treturn p.newAttempt(maxBallot(p.ballot, m.Promised))\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tif _, seen := p.accepts[m.From]; seen {\n\t\t\treturn nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tp.accepts[m.From] = struct{}{}\n\t\tif len(p.accepts) >= p.Config.Q2 {\n\t\t\tp.committed = true\n\t\t\tp.commitValue = p.chosen\n\t\t\tp.phase = phaseCommitted\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n```\n\n### `harness.go`\n\n```go\npackage flexpaxos\n\nimport \"fmt\"\n\n// This file is a deterministic, in-process message-reorder harness. It is not\n// part of the wire protocol. It schedules every request/reply at a logical\n// time chosen from a seeded PRNG, so a seed reproduces an exact interleaving.\n\ntype rng64 struct{ state uint64 }\n\nfunc newRNG(seed uint64) *rng64 {\n\tif seed == 0 {\n\t\tseed = 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15\n\t}\n\treturn &rng64{state: seed}\n}\nfunc (r *rng64) next() uint64 {\n\tx := r.state\n\tx ^= x << 13\n\tx ^= x >> 7\n\tx ^= x << 17\n\tr.state = x\n\treturn x\n}\nfunc (r *rng64) Intn(n int) int {\n\tif n <= 0 {\n\t\treturn 0\n\t}\n\treturn int(r.next() % uint64(n))\n}\n\ntype event struct {\n\tdue int64\n\tseq int64\n\tmsg Message\n}\n\n// Harness owns acceptors, proposers, and a logical-time priority queue.\ntype Harness struct {\n\tConfig    Config\n\tacceptors []*Acceptor\n\tproposers map[int]*Proposer\n\tballots   *BallotGen\n\trng       *rng64\n\tevents    []event\n\tseq       int64\n\tclock     int64\n\tdelayMax  int\n\n\tcommitted  map[int]Value\n\tviolations []string\n\tdelivered  int\n}\n\nfunc NewHarness(cfg Config, seed uint64) (*Harness, error) {\n\tif err := cfg.Validate(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\th := &Harness{\n\t\tConfig:    cfg,\n\t\tproposers: make(map[int]*Proposer),\n\t\tballots:   NewBallotGen(0),\n\t\trng:       newRNG(seed),\n\t\tdelayMax:  24,\n\t\tcommitted: make(map[int]Value),\n\t}\n\th.acceptors = make([]*Acceptor, cfg.N)\n\tfor i := range h.acceptors {\n\t\th.acceptors[i] = NewAcceptor(i)\n\t}\n\treturn h, nil\n}\n\nfunc (h *Harness) Acceptor(id int) *Acceptor { return h.acceptors[id] }\n\nfunc (h *Harness) AddProposer(id int) *Proposer {\n\tp := NewProposer(id, h.Config, h.ballots)\n\th.proposers[id] = p\n\treturn p\n}\n\nfunc (h *Harness) Start(index int, id int, value Value) {\n\tp := h.proposers[id]\n\tif p == nil {\n\t\tp = h.AddProposer(id)\n\t}\n\th.enqueueAll(p.Start(index, value))\n}\n\nfunc (h *Harness) Crash(id int) { delete(h.proposers, id) }\n\nfunc (h *Harness) Pending() bool { return len(h.events) > 0 }\n\nfunc (h *Harness) enqueueAll(msgs []Message) {\n\tfor _, m := range msgs {\n\t\th.enqueue(m)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc (h *Harness) enqueue(m Message) {\n\t// Strictly positive random delay; a larger delayMax forces deeper reorder.\n\tdue := h.clock + int64(1+h.rng.Intn(h.delayMax+1))\n\th.seq++\n\th.events = append(h.events, event{due: due, seq: h.seq, msg: m})\n}\n\nfunc (h *Harness) pop() (event, bool) {\n\tif len(h.events) == 0 {\n\t\treturn event{}, false\n\t}\n\tbest := 0\n\tfor i := 1; i < len(h.events); i++ {\n\t\tif h.events[i].due < h.events[best].due ||\n\t\t\t(h.events[i].due == h.events[best].due && h.events[i].seq < h.events[best].seq) {\n\t\t\tbest = i\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\te := h.events[best]\n\th.events = append(h.events[:best], h.events[best+1:]...)\n\treturn e, true\n}\n\n// Step delivers exactly one network event and schedules any replies.\nfunc (h *Harness) Step() bool {\n\te, ok := h.pop()\n\tif !ok {\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n\tif e.due > h.clock {\n\t\th.clock = e.due\n\t}\n\th.delivered++\n\tm := e.msg\n\tswitch m.Type {\n\tcase MsgPrepare:\n\t\tr := h.acceptors[m.To].Prepare(m.Ballot)\n\t\th.enqueue(Message{\n\t\t\tType: MsgPromise, From: m.To, To: m.From, Index: m.Index, Ballot: m.Ballot,\n\t\t\tOK: r.OK, Promised: r.Promised, AcceptedBallot: r.AcceptedBallot,\n\t\t\tAcceptedValue: r.AcceptedValue, HasAccepted: r.HasAccepted,\n\t\t})\n\tcase MsgAccept:\n\t\tr := h.acceptors[m.To].Accept(m.Ballot, m.Value)\n\t\th.enqueue(Message{\n\t\t\tType: MsgAccepted, From: m.To, To: m.From, Index: m.Index,\n\t\t\tBallot: m.Ballot, OK: r.OK, Promised: r.Promised,\n\t\t})\n\tcase MsgPromise, MsgAccepted:\n\t\tp := h.proposers[m.To]\n\t\tif p == nil {\n\t\t\treturn true // crashed proposer: its replies are lost\n\t\t}\n\t\twasCommitted := p.committed\n\t\treplies := p.Handle(m)\n\t\tif !wasCommitted && p.committed {\n\t\t\th.recordCommit(p.index, p.commitValue)\n\t\t}\n\t\th.enqueueAll(replies)\n\t}\n\treturn true\n}\n\nfunc (h *Harness) recordCommit(index int, value Value) {\n\tif old, ok := h.committed[index]; ok {\n\t\tif old != value {\n\t\t\th.violations = append(h.violations, fmt.Sprintf(\n\t\t\t\t\"divergent commit index=%d old=%q new=%q clock=%d\", index, old, value, h.clock))\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\th.committed[index] = value\n}\n\n// RunUntil delivers events until pred is true (or the queue empties/max steps\n// is reached). It returns whether pred became true.\nfunc (h *Harness) RunUntil(maxSteps int, pred func() bool) bool {\n\tfor steps := 0; steps < maxSteps; steps++ {\n\t\tif pred() {\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t}\n\t\tif !h.Step() {\n\t\t\treturn pred()\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn pred()\n}\n\nfunc (h *Harness) Run(maxSteps int) {\n\th.RunUntil(maxSteps, func() bool { return false })\n}\n\nfunc (h *Harness) Committed(index int) (Value, bool) {\n\tv, ok := h.committed[index]\n\treturn v, ok\n}\n\nfunc (h *Harness) Violations() []string { return append([]string(nil), h.violations...) }\n\nfunc (h *Harness) Check() error {\n\tif len(h.violations) > 0 {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"safety violation: %s\", h.violations[0])\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// CountAccepted returns how many acceptors durably accepted value at a ballot.\nfunc (h *Harness) CountAccepted(value Value) int {\n\tn := 0\n\tfor _, a := range h.acceptors {\n\t\t_, _, av, ok, _ := a.Snapshot()\n\t\tif ok && av == value {\n\t\t\tn++\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn n\n}\n```\n\n### Test suite (`flexpaxos_test.go`)\n\n```go\npackage flexpaxos\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\nfunc legalConfigs(maxN int) []Config {\n\tvar out []Config\n\tfor n := 2; n <= maxN; n++ {\n\t\tfor q1 := 1; q1 <= n; q1++ {\n\t\t\tfor q2 := 1; q2 <= n; q2++ {\n\t\t\t\tc := Config{N: n, Q1: q1, Q2: q2}\n\t\t\t\tif c.Validate() == nil {\n\t\t\t\t\tout = append(out, c)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn out\n}\n\nfunc TestConfigValidation(t *testing.T) {\n\tfor _, c := range legalConfigs(8) {\n\t\tif err := c.Validate(); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"legal config %+v rejected: %v\", c, err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfor _, c := range []Config{\n\t\t{N: 3, Q1: 1, Q2: 2}, // 1+2 <= 3: unsound\n\t\t{N: 5, Q1: 2, Q2: 3}, // exactly half: unsound\n\t\t{N: 4, Q1: 0, Q2: 4},\n\t\t{N: 4, Q1: 5, Q2: 1},\n\t\t{N: 0, Q1: 1, Q2: 1},\n\t} {\n\t\tif err := c.Validate(); err == nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"unsafe config %+v accepted\", c)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestLoadConfigBoundary(t *testing.T) {\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\twrite := func(name, body string) string {\n\t\tp := filepath.Join(dir, name)\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(p, []byte(body), 0o600); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn p\n\t}\n\tif _, err := LoadConfig(write(\"ok.json\", `{\"n\":5,\"q1\":2,\"q2\":4}`)); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"legal config rejected: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tif _, err := LoadConfig(write(\"bad.json\", `{\"n\":5,\"q1\":2,\"q2\":3}`)); err == nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"unsafe config q1+q2==n was accepted\")\n\t}\n\tif _, err := LoadConfig(write(\"unknown.json\", `{\"n\":5,\"q1\":2,\"q2\":4,\"mode\":\"fast\"}`)); err == nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"unknown field was accepted\")\n\t}\n}\n\n// Enumerate all possible quorum subsets for small N and check the loader's\n// inequality really is equivalent to every Q1 intersecting every Q2.\nfunc TestEveryLegalQuorumPairIntersects(t *testing.T) {\n\tfor _, c := range legalConfigs(7) {\n\t\tfor q1 := 0; q1 < (1 << c.N); q1++ {\n\t\t\tif popcount(q1) != c.Q1 {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor q2 := 0; q2 < (1 << c.N); q2++ {\n\t\t\t\tif popcount(q2) != c.Q2 {\n\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tif q1&q2 == 0 {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"config %+v has disjoint quorums %07b and %07b\", c, q1, q2)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc popcount(x int) int {\n\tn := 0\n\tfor x != 0 {\n\t\tn += x & 1\n\t\tx >>= 1\n\t}\n\treturn n\n}\n\n// Two (or three) proposers race with different values while every message is\n// independently delayed. The invariant checked is the Paxos safety property:\n// no two proposers may commit different values at the same log index.\nfunc TestSafetyAllLegalQuorumsUnderReordering(t *testing.T) {\n\tfor _, cfg := range legalConfigs(6) {\n\t\tcfg := cfg\n\t\tt.Run(fmt.Sprintf(\"n%d-q1%d-q2%d\", cfg.N, cfg.Q1, cfg.Q2), func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\t\tfor seed := uint64(0); seed < 40; seed++ {\n\t\t\t\th, err := NewHarness(cfg, seed*0x9e3779b97f4a7c15+1)\n\t\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tnProps := 2 + int(seed%2)\n\t\t\t\tfor id := 0; id < nProps; id++ {\n\t\t\t\t\th.AddProposer(id)\n\t\t\t\t\th.Start(0, id, Value(fmt.Sprintf(\"p%d\", id)))\n\t\t\t\t}\n\n\t\t\t\t// At one deterministic point, crash a proposer that may have\n\t\t\t\t// accepted a value but not yet learned that it committed, then\n\t\t\t\t// start a recovery proposer with a conflicting value.\n\t\t\t\tcrashAt := 20 + int(seed%40)\n\t\t\t\tcrashed := int(seed) % nProps\n\t\t\t\tdidCrash := false\n\t\t\t\tfor step := 0; step < 3000 && h.Pending(); step++ {\n\t\t\t\t\th.Step()\n\t\t\t\t\tif !didCrash && step == crashAt {\n\t\t\t\t\t\th.Crash(crashed)\n\t\t\t\t\t\th.AddProposer(1000 + int(seed))\n\t\t\t\t\t\th.Start(0, 1000+int(seed), Value(fmt.Sprintf(\"recovered-%d\", seed)))\n\t\t\t\t\t\tdidCrash = true\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tif err := h.Check(); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"seed %d: %v\", seed, err)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tif _, ok := h.Committed(0); !ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"seed %d: no proposer committed (config %+v)\", seed, cfg)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}\n\n// A proposer is crashed immediately after phase 1 sends its accepts. Its value\n// can therefore already be chosen by a Q2, but the crashed proposer never\n// observed Q2 replies. The recovery proposer must learn that value using only\n// Q1 promises and must not commit its own conflicting value.\nfunc TestRecoveryUsesOnlyPhase1Quorum(t *testing.T) {\n\tfor _, cfg := range legalConfigs(6) {\n\t\tcfg := cfg\n\t\tt.Run(fmt.Sprintf(\"n%d-q1%d-q2%d\", cfg.N, cfg.Q1, cfg.Q2), func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\t\tfor seed := uint64(0); seed < 20; seed++ {\n\t\t\t\th, err := NewHarness(cfg, seed+1)\n\t\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\th.AddProposer(1)\n\t\t\t\th.Start(0, 1, \"old\")\n\t\t\t\tok := h.RunUntil(2000, func() bool {\n\t\t\t\t\tp := h.proposers[1]\n\t\t\t\t\treturn p != nil && p.Phase() == phaseAccepting\n\t\t\t\t})\n\t\t\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"seed %d: old proposer never reached phase 2\", seed)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\n\t\t\t\t// Drop all replies to proposer 1, but let its phase-2 requests\n\t\t\t\t// reach the acceptors. This creates an accepted value whose\n\t\t\t\t// proposer does not know it may be committed.\n\t\t\t\th.Crash(1)\n\t\t\t\th.Run(2000)\n\t\t\t\tif got := h.CountAccepted(\"old\"); got < cfg.Q2 {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"seed %d: only %d acceptors accepted old value, need Q2=%d\", seed, got, cfg.Q2)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tif _, ok := h.Committed(0); ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"seed %d: crashed proposer observed commit unexpectedly\", seed)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\n\t\t\t\th.AddProposer(2)\n\t\t\t\th.Start(0, 2, \"new\")\n\t\t\t\th.Run(3000)\n\t\t\t\tif err := h.Check(); err != nil {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"seed %d: %v\", seed, err)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tgot, ok := h.Committed(0)\n\t\t\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"seed %d: recovery proposer did not commit\", seed)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tif got != \"old\" {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"seed %d: recovery committed %q, want recovered value %q\", seed, got, \"old\")\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tv, cok := h.proposers[2].Committed()\n\t\t\t\tif !cok || v != \"old\" {\n\t\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"seed %d: recovery proposer state = (%q,%v), want (old,true)\", seed, v, cok)\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestStaleAcceptIsRejected(t *testing.T) {\n\ta := NewAcceptor(0)\n\tb1 := Ballot{Round: 1}\n\tb2 := Ballot{Round: 2}\n\n\tif r := a.Prepare(b1); !r.OK {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"first prepare rejected\")\n\t}\n\tif r := a.Accept(b1, \"v1\"); !r.OK {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"first accept rejected\")\n\t}\n\tif r := a.Prepare(b2); !r.OK {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"higher prepare rejected\")\n\t}\n\tif r := a.Accept(b1, \"v1\"); r.OK {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"stale accept at lower ballot was accepted\")\n\t} else if r.Promised.Compare(b2) != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"reject did not advertise promised ballot: %+v\", r)\n\t}\n\t// Equal-ballot duplicate prepare remains idempotent and reports the value.\n\tif r := a.Prepare(b2); !r.OK || r.HasAccepted != true || r.AcceptedValue != \"v1\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"duplicate prepare did not return durable accepted state: %+v\", r)\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nRun the checks from the module directory:\n\n```bash\ncd flexpaxos\ngo vet ./...\ngo test ./... -count=1 -race -timeout=180s\n```\n\nVerified output:\n\n```text\nok  \texample.com/flexpaxos\t2.831s\n```\n\nThe test suite covers:\n\n* `TestConfigValidation` and `TestLoadConfigBoundary`: legal configurations pass; `Q1+Q2 <= N`, out-of-range quorums, unknown JSON fields, and trailing JSON are rejected.\n* `TestEveryLegalQuorumPairIntersects`: exhaustively enumerates every quorum subset for `N <= 7` and proves there is no disjoint `Q1`/`Q2` pair for any config accepted by the loader.\n* `TestSafetyAllLegalQuorumsUnderReordering`: for every legal config with `N <= 6`, 40 seeded schedules run two or three proposers with different values, independent random message delays, one proposer crash, and a conflicting recovery proposer. The harness records every commit and fails on the first time two different values are committed at index 0.\n* `TestRecoveryUsesOnlyPhase1Quorum`: crashes a proposer after it has sent phase-2 accepts but before it observes any phase-2 reply, so the value may already be chosen without the proposer knowing. A recovery proposer with a conflicting value must learn the old value from Q1 promises and commit it.\n* `TestStaleAcceptIsRejected`: an accept below the promised ballot is rejected; an idempotent duplicate prepare returns the durable accepted state.\n\nOperational note: in a real process, persist the acceptor state and the `BallotGen.last` value (or initialize the generator from the maximum ballot found on disk) before acknowledging messages. Leases may optimize the fast path, but a lease must never allow bypassing the ballot promise fence; otherwise an expired/stale leader can violate the same intersection argument.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: go-flexible-paxos-fast-quorum-recovery-ballot-safety\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-24T22:04:53.771Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Implement a Flexible Paxos replica: phase-1 quorum Q1 and phase-2 quorum Q2 are configured independently and safety holds only if every Q1 intersects every Q2 (|Q1|+|Q2| > N), so a fixed full-majority check is simultaneously unsound for some configs and needlessly rejecting for others. Build the acceptor state machine (promised ballot, accepted ballot/value, recovery ballot) plus a proposer that recovers a possibly-committed value after a crash using only Q1 responses, tolerating stale and reordered messages, ballot/lease conflicts, and two proposers with interleaved ballots that each believe they hold a Q2 majority. Ship a deterministic message-reorder harness proving no two proposers commit different values at the same log index for any legal quorum configuration, and make the config loader reject any Q1+Q2 <= N.", "environment": "go1.26", "language": "go", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "go-flexible-paxos-fast-quorum-recovery-ballot-safety", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-24T22:04:53.774Z", "version": "1.26"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-24 22:04:53"}
+{"class_id": 2971, "class": "node20-btree-suffix-truncation-minimal-separator-split", "title": "node20-btree-suffix-truncation-minimal-separator-split", "description": "node20-btree-suffix-truncation-minimal-separator-split", "answer_id": 4703, "language": "js", "environment": "node20", "version": "20", "solution": "# B+tree Prefix Compression + Minimal Separator Split\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe failure class comes from treating an internal separator as “the first key of the right page” (or as a copied full key) instead of a value in the legal interval\n\n```text\nlast_key(left) < separator <= first_key(right)\n```\n\nThat causes several related failures:\n\n1. **No true suffix truncation**\n   - A separator that copies the entire right key is legal but not the shortest separator.\n   - A truncated separator that is not strictly greater than the left page’s last key breaks routing and ordering.\n\n2. **All-equal-prefix split is mishandled**\n   - If `leftLast` is a prefix of `rightFirst`, no proper prefix of `rightFirst` is greater than `leftLast`.\n   - The shortest legal separator is `leftLast + 0x00`.\n   - Using `leftLast` directly violates the strict lower bound. Using `rightFirst` is legal but not minimal.\n\n3. **Stale separators after boundary changes**\n   - Inserting/deleting a new minimum or maximum in a child changes the boundary represented by its parent separator.\n   - Recomputing only during splits leaves “legal but not shortest” or even out-of-interval separators after insertion/deletion/borrow/merge.\n\n4. **Rightmost/sibling metadata not repaired**\n   - Splitting the rightmost leaf must clear the old leaf’s `rightmost` flag, set it on the new leaf, and update the tree’s `rightmostLeaf`.\n   - Splitting the root must create a new root and assign `rightmost` correctly to the left/right pages.\n   - Splitting an internal rightmost page must propagate the rightmost flag to the new right sibling.\n\n5. **Decode-side validation too weak**\n   - Reconstructing `pagePrefix + keySuffix` must be followed by a strict ordering check.\n   - Truncated separators that reconstruct to duplicate or decreasing keys must be rejected.\n   - Lengths, child counts, and trailing bytes must be checked to avoid accepting corrupt pages.\n\n---\n\n## Exact fix\n\nThe complete verified implementation is below.\n\nKey points:\n\n- `minimalSeparator` computes the shortest legal separator and handles the prefix/sentinel case.\n- Pages store one `prefixLen` plus one `truncLen` per key.\n- `decodePage` reconstructs keys and rejects non-increasing ordering.\n- `_refreshSeparator` recomputes a parent separator from the current child boundaries.\n- `_refreshAncestors` propagates boundary repairs only along leftmost/rightmost paths.\n- Splits repair `next`/`prev`, `rightmost`, and the root/rightmost-leaf pointers.\n- Deletes implement borrow/merge at both leaf and internal levels while maintaining separators.\n\n### `btree.js`\n\n```js\n'use strict';\n\n/*\n * btree.js -- B+tree with prefix-compressed pages and shortest legal\n * (suffix-truncated) internal separators.\n *\n * Page codec format:\n *\n *   magic         4 bytes  \"BTPC\"\n *   kind          1 byte   0 = leaf, 1 = internal\n *   flags         1 byte   bit 0 = rightmost page flag\n *   count         2 bytes  number of keys\n *   pageId        4 bytes\n *   prefixLen     4 bytes  page-level shared prefix length\n *   prefix        prefixLen bytes\n *   entries       count times:\n *                   truncLen 4 bytes\n *                   suffix   truncLen bytes\n *                   if leaf: valueLen 4 bytes, value bytes\n *   childIds      only for internal: (count + 1) * 4 bytes\n *\n * `truncLen` is the per-key truncation length after the page prefix.\n * Leaves retain complete keys; internal keys are shortest legal separators.\n */\n\nconst MAGIC = Buffer.from('BTPC');\nconst LEAF = 0;\nconst INTERNAL = 1;\nconst HEADER_SIZE = 4 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 4 + 4; // 16\n\nlet NEXT_PAGE_ID = 1;\n\nclass CodecError extends Error {\n  constructor(message) {\n    super(message);\n    this.name = 'CodecError';\n  }\n}\n\nfunction toBuffer(value) {\n  if (Buffer.isBuffer(value)) return Buffer.from(value);\n  if (typeof value === 'string') return Buffer.from(value, 'utf8');\n  if (value instanceof Uint8Array) return Buffer.from(value);\n  throw new TypeError('key/value must be Buffer, Uint8Array, or string');\n}\n\nfunction compareBytes(a, b) {\n  return Buffer.compare(a, b);\n}\n\nfunction commonPrefixLength(a, b) {\n  const n = Math.min(a.length, b.length);\n  let i = 0;\n  while (i < n && a[i] === b[i]) i++;\n  return i;\n}\n\nfunction pagePrefix(keys) {\n  if (keys.length === 0) return Buffer.alloc(0);\n  let p = keys[0];\n  for (let i = 1; i < keys.length && p.length; i++) {\n    p = p.subarray(0, commonPrefixLength(p, keys[i]));\n  }\n  return Buffer.from(p);\n}\n\n/*\n * Shortest key S with left < S <= right.\n *\n * If the keys first differ at byte i, right[0..i] is the shortest key in\n * the allowed interval. If left is a prefix of right, no proper prefix of\n * right is > left, so append the smallest sentinel byte (0x00). This is the\n * all-equal-prefix case.\n */\nfunction minimalSeparator(left, right) {\n  if (compareBytes(left, right) >= 0) {\n    throw new Error('minimalSeparator requires left < right');\n  }\n  const n = Math.min(left.length, right.length);\n  let i = 0;\n  while (i < n && left[i] === right[i]) i++;\n\n  if (i < left.length && i < right.length) {\n    // First differing byte. right[i] > left[i].\n    return Buffer.from(right.subarray(0, i + 1));\n  }\n\n  // left is a proper prefix of right. left + 0x00 is strictly > left and\n  // is <= right (if right is exactly left + 0x00 it is equal, which is legal).\n  if (i === left.length && i < right.length) {\n    const out = Buffer.alloc(left.length + 1);\n    left.copy(out, 0);\n    out[left.length] = 0x00;\n    return out;\n  }\n\n  throw new Error('unreachable minimalSeparator state');\n}\n\nfunction writeU32(buf, off, v) {\n  buf.writeUInt32BE(v >>> 0, off);\n}\nfunction readU32(buf, off) {\n  return buf.readUInt32BE(off);\n}\n\nfunction encodePage(page) {\n  const keys = page.keys;\n  const prefix = pagePrefix(keys);\n  const kind = page.leaf ? LEAF : INTERNAL;\n  const count = keys.length;\n  if (count > 0xffff) throw new CodecError('too many keys on page');\n\n  const items = [];\n  let bodyLen = 0;\n  for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {\n    const suffix = keys[i].subarray(prefix.length);\n    if (page.leaf) {\n      const value = page.values[i];\n      const size = 4 + suffix.length + 4 + value.length;\n      const item = Buffer.allocUnsafe(size);\n      writeU32(item, 0, suffix.length);\n      suffix.copy(item, 4);\n      writeU32(item, 4 + suffix.length, value.length);\n      value.copy(item, 8 + suffix.length);\n      items.push(item);\n      bodyLen += item.length;\n    } else {\n      const size = 4 + suffix.length;\n      const item = Buffer.allocUnsafe(size);\n      writeU32(item, 0, suffix.length);\n      suffix.copy(item, 4);\n      items.push(item);\n      bodyLen += item.length;\n    }\n  }\n\n  const childBytes = page.leaf ? 0 : (count + 1) * 4;\n  const out = Buffer.allocUnsafe(HEADER_SIZE + prefix.length + bodyLen + childBytes);\n  MAGIC.copy(out, 0);\n  out[4] = kind;\n  out[5] = page.rightmost ? 1 : 0;\n  out.writeUInt16BE(count, 6);\n  writeU32(out, 8, page.id || 0);\n  writeU32(out, 12, prefix.length);\n  prefix.copy(out, HEADER_SIZE);\n\n  let off = HEADER_SIZE + prefix.length;\n  for (const item of items) {\n    item.copy(out, off);\n    off += item.length;\n  }\n\n  if (!page.leaf) {\n    if (page.children.length !== count + 1) {\n      throw new CodecError('internal page must have count + 1 children');\n    }\n    off = HEADER_SIZE + prefix.length + bodyLen;\n    for (const child of page.children) {\n      writeU32(out, off, child.id || 0);\n      off += 4;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return out;\n}\n\nfunction decodePage(buf